# Mobile — section chrome cache (chips / tabs)

Do **not** add server columns, list-DTO booleans, or extra list joins to decide which
podcast/channel/item chips exist. Visibility that the list payload does not already carry is
learned on the **detail** query and stored **on the device**.

## Always-on vs evidence chips

Some chips are always offered; an empty list is a real answer. Evidence chips appear only when
this entity has been seen to carry that content.

**Podcast** always-on (in this order): Episodes, Downloaded, About, Clips.

**Podcast** evidence chips (after the always-on set): Official Clips, Podroll, and later Boosts
or anything else that would pop in after the detail DTO. Put every evidence chip at the **end**
of the horizontal row so a first-visit insert is more likely off-screen on a phone.

**Episode** always-on: Summary, Clips. Evidence chips last: Chapters, Official Clips, Transcript.

## First paint

1. Read the in-memory / SQLite chrome cache for this channel or item (`sectionChromeFlags`).
2. Paint always-on chips plus any evidence chips the cache already recorded.
3. After the detail query (and the local soundbites scan), confirm visibility and write the
   cache. The list body may spin; the chip row must not wait on the network when the cache
   already knows.

A first-ever open of an entity with no cache row still adds evidence chips when the DTO
arrives. That flash stays at the end of the row. Do not hide the whole chip row to avoid it.
Do not prefetch `GET /channel/:id` or `GET /item/:id` on list press to populate the cache.

## Storage

Device-local only (`section_chrome_flags` + the in-memory map). Do not sync these flags to the
account. Do not denormalize them onto Postgres `channel` / `item`.

